<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neoplatonism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neoplatonist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neoplatonism is the name modern scholars give to the late form of Platonic philosophy founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> in the third century CE. It taught that all things emanate from a single first principle, the One, and that the soul can return to it through philosophy and contemplation. Developed by Porphyry, Iamblichus and Proclus, it deeply influenced Christian, Jewish and Islamic thought and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neoplatonism.jpg" alt="Neoplatonism" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_sarcophagus_of_a_reader_identified_to_Plotinus_and_disciples.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Neoplatonism is a modern term. It marks off the doctrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> and his successors from those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, and assumes that Plotinus's reading of Plato was new. Some scholars doubt that the label is useful, arguing that only marginal differences separate Plotinus from his immediate predecessors. In the nearly six centuries between Plato and Plotinus there had been an uninterrupted tradition of interpreting Plato, including the period now called Middle Platonism. Among the forerunners were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, the Neopythagorean Numenius of Apamea and the Hellenized Jew Philo. Plotinus was the pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas">Ammonius Saccas</a>, whom Christians and pagans each claimed as an adherent of their own faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Its central idea is monism: all reality derives from a single first principle, the One. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> the One is simple, ineffable and beyond being, both the source and the end of all things, and the most adequate names for it are the One or the Good. From it emanate two further hypostases: Nous, or intellect, which contains the Platonic Forms, and Soul, whose actualising of what it receives from Nous is the creation of the sensible world. Evil has no independent existence but is an absence of good. Human happiness can be reached in this world through philosophical contemplation, and all souls return to the One.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> came three phases: Porphyry, who edited the Enneads and wrote a biography of his teacher; Iamblichus, who filled the space between the One and humanity with gods, angels and demons and made salvation a matter of theurgy, or rites; and the 5th and 6th centuries, when the academies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> flourished. Proclus placed individual henads, identified with the Greek gods, between the One and the divine Intellect. A revived academy at Athens, founded around 410, was closed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> in 529 because of the active paganism of its professors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> passed through Neoplatonism on his way from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and it led him to hold that evil is a privation of good and that God is not material. Islamic philosophers such as al-Farabi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> adapted it to Islamic monotheism, aided by Arabic translations, and Jewish thinkers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> ibn Gabirol and the Kabbalists used it too. In the West the works of Pseudo-Dionysius shaped medieval mysticism, including Meister Eckhart, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> drew on Proclus. Pletho carried its revival to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a>, which led to the Neoplatonism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a>. In the 17th century the Cambridge Platonists, among them Henry More, were called more truly Plotinists by Coleridge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neoplatonism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Claudius Ptolemy wrote the Almagest and the Geography there in the second century, Plotinus studied under the philosopher Ammonius Saccas before founding <b>Neoplatonism</b> in Rome, and Hero of Alexandria described machines and devices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The king welcomed <b>Neoplatonist</b> philosophers who left Athens after Justinian's closure of the Neoplatonic Academy in 529 CE, sponsored translations from Greek and Sanskrit, and encouraged medical learning at Gundishapur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The faith grew from the Ismāʿīlī branch of Shiʿa Islam and shows influences of Christianity, Gnosticism, <b>Neoplatonism</b>, Zoroastrianism, Manichaeism and Pythagoreanism.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
